OSKit Arch — TRISC CPU Primitives

Architecture-specific CPU operations for TRISC: interrupt
enable/disable and thread stack frame construction.

    module oskit.arch

Offset from saved SSP to the return-value register (r1)
in the context frame. Used by syscall_return to write the
result where restore_thread will pop it into r1.
TRISC layout: PSR(0), PC(8), r1(16), ... so offset=16.

Wait, let me verify. The TRISC context frame is saved
in boot.asm as: USP, r6, r5, r4, r3, r2, r1, PC, PSR
(pushed in that order, so PSR is at top/lowest address).
SSP points at PSR. r1 is at SSP + 6*8 = 48.

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 48

Disable interrupts. Must be called before accessing
shared kernel state (run queues, thread table, etc.).

    arch_cli()
        asm("cli")

Enable interrupts. Called after kernel state updates
are complete, allowing the timer and device IRQs to fire.

    arch_sti()
        asm("sti")

Build a fake context frame on the supervisor stack so
that restore_thread in boot.asm can pop it and RTE into
the new thread. TRISC layout (9 fields, top to bottom):
PSR, PC, r1, r2, r3, r4, r5, r6, USP

    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 72        // 9 fields × 8 bytes (PSR, PC, r1-r6, USP)

    extern thread_exit: byte

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        var sp = ssp
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 2                 // PSR: Mode=1 (supervisor), Ind=0 (interrupts on)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // PC
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r1
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r2
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r3
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r4
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r5
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(&thread_exit) // r6 = return address (exit trampoline)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = usp               // USP
        sp
